= The long-term effects of opioid use before joint replacement surgery: A dose-response trial emulation with New Zealand linked register data. Analysis plan
#smallcaps[Published]\ 08 September 2023

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Pryymachenko Y, Wilson R, Abbott JH, Choong P, Dowsey M. The long-term effects of opioid use before joint replacement surgery: A dose-response trial emulation with New Zealand linked register data. Analysis plan. OSF, 08 September 2023. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/g52kt\")[10.17605/osf.io/g52kt]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
Osteoarthritis (OA) is a common chronic health condition, affecting more
than 500 million people worldwide in 2019. Opioids are commonly used to manage
pain associated with OA, and their use is usually the highest before a total joint
replacement (TJR) surgery due to more severe joint pain. However, patients receiving
opioids before surgery respond worse to pain relief following surgery, have higher
risk of surgical complications, revision surgery, and prolonged opioid use post-surgery.
Pre-TJR opioid use also negatively affects long-term health and socio-economic
outcomes. Although the need to minimize pre-surgery opioid use is evident, it
has proven to be challenging.

While opioids are still widely used for pre-TJR
pain management, and particularly for patients with medical contraindications
of nonopioid alternatives, it is important to establish the level of opioid use
at which their negative effect manifests itself (if such a level exists). The
aim of this study is to investigate the dose-response relationship between pre-TJR
opioid use and long-term health and socio-economic outcomes.

